# Plan Rules

Two document types, two audiences. Keep them separate.

## Document Type Selection

- Design (`-design.md`) — Team review; architecture decision record; onboarding context. SA-level.
- Plan (`-plan.md`) — AI agent or developer will implement directly from this document. SD-level.
- Both — the standard for any meaningful feature: design captures the "why", plan translates it into steps.

Design always comes first. Plan follows — it is only meaningful once the design decisions are settled.
Create a plan alone only when the design is already captured elsewhere (e.g., a pre-existing design document or a trivial change with no architectural decision to record).

> Existing `docs/design/` and `docs/plan/` directories, and flat `docs/` root files, are legacy. New documents go in `docs/sys/<feature-name>/`.

## Document Location

Scope determines where the document lives:

- **Single submodule** — domain is entirely within one submodule: place under that submodule's `docs/sys/<feature-name>/`.
- **Cross-submodule** — feature spans two or more submodules: place under root `docs/sys/<feature-name>/`.

Root documents may link to submodule documents. Submodule documents MUST NOT reference root documents — each submodule is unaware of root.

## File Naming

Documents are rooted at `docs/sys/<feature-name>/`. A directory may contain both design/plan files and subdirectories; subdirectories represent sub-modules of the feature and may be nested to any depth.

File name format (applies at any directory level):

```
<name>-<6digit>-design.md
<name>-<6digit>-plan-<6digit>.md
<name>-<6digit>-plan-<6digit>.<3digit>.md
```

- `<name>` — matches the containing directory name exactly; **only alphanumerics and underscores (`[a-zA-Z0-9_]`) are allowed** — no hyphens or other special characters.
- `<6digit>` — dependency code; encodes execution order and parallelism at the current directory level (see rules below).
- `plan-<6digit>` — dependency code for the plan file; same semantics as the design dependency code.
- `[.<3digit>]` — optional; starting from `.001`, splits a large plan under the same dependency code into sequential parts (ordering only — no dependency encoding).

Directory names also MUST NOT use hyphens. This ensures that joining any file's ancestor directory names with `-` produces its unique flat name — the directory hierarchy is preserved both in the path and recoverable from the flat name.

No dates in filenames — use git tags to track when a document was added, modified, or removed.

Example:

```
docs/sys/jackpot/
  jackpot-100000-design.md          # design at this level
  jackpot-100000-plan-100000.md       # main plan (dependency code 100000)
  jackpot-100000-plan-100000.001.md  # first SBE group (sequential, no dep encoding)
  jackpot-100000-plan-100000.002.md  # second SBE group
  trigger/                          # trigger sub-module directory
    trigger-100000-plan.md          # flat name: jackpot-trigger-100000-plan.md
  deduction/                        # deduction sub-module directory
    deduction-100000-design.md
    deduction-100000-plan.md
    history/                        # can be nested further
      history-100000-plan.md        # flat name: jackpot-deduction-history-100000-plan.md
```

6-digit dependency code rules:
- Tasks at the same digit position but different values are independent (`100000` ‖ `200000`).
- A task depends on (must execute after) any task whose code shares its non-zero prefix but has additional non-zero digits: `100000` waits for `110000` and `120000`.
- Execute from the most specific (fewest trailing zeros) upward.
- For purely sequential work with no parallelism, use `100000`, `200000`, `300000`, etc.

## Design Document — `docs/sys/`

Target reader: any human team member — developer, architect, or new contributor. SA-level document.

Read `references/design-template.md` for the full template.

Core rules:
- Traditional Chinese throughout; no code blocks, no struct definitions
- Explain the **why**, not the how
- Each non-obvious decision must have a rationale entry
- Mermaid diagrams are welcome for flows and context maps
- Target 500 lines maximum; if too large, split into additional design documents using a subname (see File Naming)

## Plan Document — `docs/sys/`

Target reader: AI agent executing the implementation. SD-level document.

Read `references/plan-template.md` for the full template.

Core rules:
- Traditional Chinese prose; English for all identifiers (package paths, type
  names, function names, field names)
- Precision over readability — every ambiguity forces the agent to guess
- Go struct definitions in fenced code blocks with `go` language tag
- No tables, no Mermaid diagrams — plan docs are AI agent documents; follow
  the same write-md rules that apply to SKILL.md files
- Non-goals section is **mandatory** — explicitly state what is out of scope
- Every behavioral spec must satisfy SBE (see below)
- Target 500 lines maximum; if too large, split using a subname or the plan serial suffix; all plan files use the 6-digit dependency code (see File Naming)

## SBE Enforcement

Every behavior defined in a plan document must have at least one concrete
example. An abstract description with no example is considered undefined scope.

### Example format

```
### Behavior name

**Input**
- fieldA: value
- fieldB: value

**Output**
- fieldX: value
- fieldY: value

**Edge cases**
- nil input → return nil, no error
- zero baseBet → skip, contribution = 0
```

### Completeness checklist

Before finalizing any plan document, verify for each behavior:

- [ ] At least one concrete input/output example exists
- [ ] Boundary values are covered (nil, zero, max, empty slice)
- [ ] All failure/skip paths are explicit — no "TBD" or "handle appropriately"
- [ ] An agent reading this alone could implement without clarification questions

A plan that fails this checklist is a draft, not a spec.

## Markdown Formatting

Follow the `write-md` skill for general Markdown rules. Additional constraints:
- Plan docs: `##` for major sections, `###` for individual behaviors
- Prefer bullet lists over prose paragraphs for requirements
- Add a quick-nav TOC if the document has five or more `##` sections
